@page "/thanks"
@layout MainLayout
@inject IBreadcrumbTrail Trail

<PageTitle>Thanks &amp; Sources &mdash; Respondeo</PageTitle>

<SeoHead Title="Thanks & Sources"
         Description="The inspiration behind Respondeo, the public-domain and open sources it draws on, and further reading we recommend for exploring the Catholic faith." />

<section class="thanks">
    <header class="pillar__header">
        <p class="pillar__eyebrow">Thanks &amp; Sources</p>
        <h1 class="pillar__title">With Gratitude</h1>
        <p class="pillar__lede">
            Respondeo stands on the shoulders of saints, scholars, and stewards who preserved and
            shared the treasures of the faith. This page names the inspiration behind the project,
            the sources it draws on, and further reading we warmly recommend.
        </p>
    </header>

    <div class="thanks__body">
        <section class="thanks__section">
            <h2 class="thanks__heading">Our Inspiration</h2>
            <p>
                The project takes its name and spirit from St. Thomas Aquinas, whose
                <i>Summa Theologiae</i> answers real questions with patient, ordered reason. Every
                article of the <i>Summa</i> opens by taking objections seriously, then replies
                &mdash; <i>respondeo dicendum</i>, &ldquo;I answer that.&rdquo; That posture, meeting
                honest doubt with charity and clarity, is the model this site tries to follow.
            </p>
            <p>
                Respondeo is presented in that spirit: real questions, reasoned answers, always
                pointing beyond itself to the living faith of the Catholic Church and the guidance
                of her Magisterium.
            </p>
            <p>
                We owe a particular debt to
                <a href="https://aquinas101.thomisticinstitute.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aquinas 101</a>,
                a project of the Thomistic Institute. Their video series and their far more robust
                online <i>Summa</i> have been a major inspiration for this site, and we recommend
                them wholeheartedly to anyone who wants to go deeper.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="thanks__section">
            <h2 class="thanks__heading">Sources We Rely On</h2>
            <p>These public-domain and freely shared works make the content here possible.</p>
            <ul class="thanks__list">
                <li>
                    <span class="thanks__item-title">Summa Theologiae</span> &mdash; English
                    translation by the Fathers of the English Dominican Province (Benziger Bros.
                    edition, 1947), in the public domain. Source text from the
                    <a href="https://ccel.org/ccel/a/aquinas/summa/cache/summa.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Christian Classics Ethereal Library (CCEL)</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="thanks__item-title">Christian Classics Ethereal Library</span>
                    &mdash; a public-domain digital library of Christian texts hosted at
                    <a href="https://ccel.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ccel.org</a>.
                </li>
                @* TODO: add the remaining sources actually used by the content manifests
                   (prayer texts, miracle references, article citations) as they are confirmed. *@
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="thanks__section">
            <h2 class="thanks__heading">Further Reading</h2>
            <p>
                Respondeo is a starting point, not a substitute for the Church's own voice. For
                deeper study we recommend these authoritative and time-tested resources.
            </p>
            <ul class="thanks__list">
                <li>
                    <span class="thanks__item-title">Catechism of the Catholic Church</span> &mdash;
                    the Church's official summary of doctrine, available at
                    <a href="https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/_INDEX.HTM" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">vatican.va</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="thanks__item-title">The Holy Bible</span> &mdash; the sacred
                    Scriptures, foundation of all that follows.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="thanks__item-title">Aquinas 101</span> &mdash; free video courses
                    and a rich online <i>Summa</i> from the Thomistic Institute, at
                    <a href="https://aquinas101.thomisticinstitute.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">aquinas101.thomisticinstitute.org</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="thanks__item-title">Capturing Catholicism</span> &mdash; thoughtful
                    apologetics videos with an especially strong treatment of miracles, on
                    <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@@CapturingCatholicism" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="thanks__item-title">Pints with Aquinas</span> &mdash; an engaging,
                    approachable introduction to St. Thomas Aquinas that first drew us in more
                    deeply; a fun way for anyone to learn more, at
                    <a href="https://pintswithaquinas.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">pintswithaquinas.com</a>.
                </li>
                @* TODO: add further recommended resources (e.g. Church Fathers, apologetics,
                   devotional classics) once the curated list is finalized. *@
            </ul>
        </section>

        <p class="thanks__closing">
            If we have overlooked a source that ought to be credited here, please
            <a href="https://github.com/Delubear/Respondeo/issues/new/choose" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">let us know</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        await Trail.ClearAsync();
    }
}
